  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Muscular dystrophy is an inherited, progressive and degenerative, generalized myopathy. Most muscular dystrophies are due to mutations in genes that code for muscle membrane associated proteins in the dystrophin–",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "glycoprotein (DAG) complex.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most common form in dogs occurs as a result of a deficiency of the protein dystrophin.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Muscular dystrophy due to dystrophin deficiency has an X-linked mode of inheritance and affected animals are predominantly male.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dystrophin deficiency—identified first in golden retrievers. Subsequently, cases have been reported in many other dog breeds.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sporadic cases in other breeds are likely.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The dystrophin gene is large and new mutations are",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "likely to occur.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypertrophic muscular dystrophy associated with dystrophin deficiency also occurs in domestic shorthair",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Deficiency of other muscle-associated proteins, e.g., alpha2 laminin (merosin) and sarcoglycan, has been identified more recently in dogs and cats and is not necessarily X-linked",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "(females affected).",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A myopathy associated with loss of alpha-dystroglycan with an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance occurs in young Devon Rex and Sphynx cats. Alphadystroglycan expression, which is associated withCOLQanchorage at the motor end-plate, has been shown to be deficient in affected cats, resulting in a congenital myasthenic syndrome.",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Unclassified muscular dystrophies have also been reported in other dogs (males and females).",
              "pdf_page": 1020,
              "printed_page": 938,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
